WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.480 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Your class is designing a miniature discovery museum.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:11.200 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The model buildings must fold from cardboard, visitors need
a reliable map, and a pair of displays must have a planned
relationship.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:17.667 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Can you connect several geometry ideas into one design that
another team could actually build and check?

00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:18.067 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are learning to combine nets, coordinates and
transformations in a justified design.

00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:29.163 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will build an explanation using models, rather than
memorising a rule without understanding it.

00:00:29.163 --> 00:00:34.843 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to combine nets, coordinates and
transformations in a justified design.

00:00:34.843 --> 00:00:40.508 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
As you investigate, connect what you can see, what you can
make, and the mathematical language you use.

00:00:40.508 --> 00:00:42.988 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
First, I can connect a net to a model.

00:00:42.988 --> 00:00:46.148 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Second, I can locate and transform a display.

00:00:46.148 --> 00:00:49.788 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Third, I can check and explain a complete spatial plan.

00:00:49.788 --> 00:00:53.306 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep these goals in mind as you explain each discovery.

00:00:53.306 --> 00:00:54.106 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Before drawing a polished plan, identify what each
representation will tell you.

00:00:54.106 --> 00:01:01.226 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A net describes the faces and joins of a model building.

00:01:01.226 --> 00:01:04.946 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A coordinate map records positions.

00:01:04.946 --> 00:01:08.986 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Transformations describe precise changes to a display shape.

00:01:08.986 --> 00:01:15.581 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each representation answers a different question, so we
should not expect one drawing to replace all the others.

00:01:15.581 --> 00:01:15.821 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our first building is a rectangular prism measuring four by
three by two centimetres.

00:01:15.821 --> 00:01:21.461 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use the matching opposite-face pairs from our net
investigation.

00:01:21.461 --> 00:01:30.701 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The top and bottom are four by three, the long sides four by
two, and the ends three by two.

00:01:30.701 --> 00:01:33.926 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check both sizes and connections before folding.

00:01:33.926 --> 00:01:37.326 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A square-based pyramid can mark the information point.

00:01:37.326 --> 00:01:42.286 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Its net needs one square base and four suitable matching
triangular faces.

00:01:42.286 --> 00:01:49.926 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It is not a triangular prism: the triangular sides meet at
an apex rather than connecting two matching parallel ends.

00:01:49.926 --> 00:01:53.977 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Explain that structural difference to the team constructing
the model.

00:01:53.977 --> 00:01:58.657 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose one origin, label the axes and use equal one-unit
intervals.

00:01:58.657 --> 00:02:02.737 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our entrance is at one, one and the main exhibit is at five,
four.

00:02:02.737 --> 00:02:05.897 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
These pairs specify positions, not journeys.

00:02:05.897 --> 00:02:13.620 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Every team must use the same horizontal-first convention, or
the museum labels could point visitors towards the wrong
places.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:18.299 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
From the entrance at one, one, move four grid spaces right
to five, one.

00:02:18.299 --> 00:02:21.139 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Then move three spaces up to five, four.

00:02:21.139 --> 00:02:23.620 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The route uses seven grid units.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:31.570 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Record the turn as well as the destination so that another
team can follow the instructions without needing to copy
your finger movements.

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:37.170 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Could a visitor travel north first and still reach the main
exhibit in seven grid units?

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:42.450 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict the intermediate coordinate after the first
movement, then the final coordinate.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:50.600 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Explain why a different-looking route can have the same
length when it uses the same horizontal and vertical changes
without backtracking.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:58.880 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Moving three units north first reaches one, four.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:02.120 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Moving four units east then reaches five, four.

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:04.920 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Three plus four still gives seven grid units.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:10.960 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The intermediate location differs from our first route,
while the destination and total distance agree.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:15.858 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
State each of these comparisons separately rather than
calling the routes identical.

00:03:15.858 --> 00:03:20.338 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our triangular display has vertices at one, two; two, two;
and one, three.

00:03:20.338 --> 00:03:24.058 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We want a matching display four units right and one unit up.

00:03:24.058 --> 00:03:26.858 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Apply this instruction to every vertex.

00:03:26.858 --> 00:03:33.762 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A translation preserves side lengths, angles and
orientation, so no corner should follow its own different
movement.

00:03:33.762 --> 00:03:37.762 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The new vertices are five, three; six, three; and five,
four.

00:03:37.762 --> 00:03:40.962 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Compare each pair of corresponding positions.

00:03:40.962 --> 00:03:46.522 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Every horizontal value increased by four and every vertical
value increased by one.

00:03:46.522 --> 00:03:53.789 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Join the new vertices in order, and keep the original
outline so the unchanged geometry remains easy to inspect.

00:03:53.789 --> 00:03:59.029 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One team redraws the triangle larger because it looks more
impressive on the map.

00:03:59.029 --> 00:04:01.669 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That may be a design choice, but it is not the requested
translation.

00:04:01.669 --> 00:04:06.709 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The instruction requires the same size and shape.

00:04:06.709 --> 00:04:12.967 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Correct the individual vertex positions using the shared
movement, rather than stretching a side by eye.

00:04:12.967 --> 00:04:18.967 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now reflect the translated triangle across the vertical line
at horizontal coordinate four.

00:04:18.967 --> 00:04:23.767 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Its vertex five, three is one unit right of that line, so
its partner is three, three.

00:04:23.767 --> 00:04:27.807 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The vertex six, three is two units right and reflects to
two, three.

00:04:27.807 --> 00:04:30.200 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check each distance independently.

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:33.520 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The remaining vertex five, four reflects to three, four.

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:39.800 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
All matching pairs keep their vertical coordinates and lie
equally far from the mirror on opposite sides.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:44.360 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This movement reverses their facing relationship while
preserving size.

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:50.560 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It is different from the earlier slide, which used one
shared displacement without reversing the orientation.

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:55.960 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A decorative marker lies at four, two, directly on the same
vertical mirror line.

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:58.400 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Where will its reflected partner be?

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:03.600 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict whether it moves at all, and explain your answer
using distance from the line.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:09.305 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This is a useful boundary case for checking whether our
reflection reasoning is consistent.

00:05:14.305 --> 00:05:16.425 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The marker remains at four, two.

00:05:16.425 --> 00:05:22.585 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Its distance from the mirror is zero, so the matching point
on the other side is still the same position.

00:05:22.585 --> 00:05:26.785 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A reflection can keep points on its mirror fixed while
moving other points.

00:05:26.785 --> 00:05:31.872 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Do not force every point to travel simply because a
transformation was requested.

00:05:31.872 --> 00:05:36.192 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For a square direction sign, investigate a full turn about
its centre.

00:05:36.192 --> 00:05:41.512 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It matches its original outline four times, including the
final full-turn match.

00:05:41.512 --> 00:05:44.392 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That is order-four rotational symmetry.

00:05:44.392 --> 00:05:47.432 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A non-square rectangular sign has order two.

00:05:47.432 --> 00:05:52.364 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The different side relationships explain why their exact
matching turns differ.

00:05:52.364 --> 00:05:56.804 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The museum labels must distinguish flat face shapes from
whole objects.

00:05:56.804 --> 00:06:01.044 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A square face belongs to a cube, but a cube is not itself a
square.

00:06:01.044 --> 00:06:07.804 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use faces, edges and vertices to describe solids, and
appropriate side-and-angle properties for flat shapes.

00:06:07.804 --> 00:06:12.085 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Precise vocabulary makes the model instructions easier to
follow.

00:06:12.085 --> 00:06:15.245 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Plan a small visitor route starting at two, two.

00:06:15.245 --> 00:06:18.005 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Move three units right and two units up.

00:06:18.005 --> 00:06:23.525 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Work out the endpoint, then add an original shape and a
translated partner elsewhere on the grid.

00:06:23.525 --> 00:06:28.925 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Include a suitable building net and explain at least one
check for each part of your design.

00:06:28.925 --> 00:06:35.965 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The visitor finishes at five, four: the horizontal
coordinate increases by three and the vertical coordinate by
two.

00:06:35.965 --> 00:06:43.485 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now inspect your other choices.

00:06:43.485 --> 00:06:43.685 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Are net edges compatible?

00:06:43.685 --> 00:06:44.445 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Do translated vertices share one movement?

00:06:44.445 --> 00:06:47.245 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Are reflected points equally far from the specified line?

00:06:47.245 --> 00:06:51.508 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A complete design needs evidence for every mathematical
decision.

00:06:51.508 --> 00:06:57.068 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You have connected flat nets, solid models, coordinate
locations, routes and transformations.

00:06:57.068 --> 00:07:02.468 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Return to the learning goal and explain which representation
solved each design problem.

00:07:02.468 --> 00:07:06.628 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Share one convincing check and one improvement you made
after testing.

00:07:06.628 --> 00:07:12.297 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Your final plan should help another team understand not just
what to build, but why it works.
